--- title
# Velis — Local-First Collaboration Architecture

--- body
Offline-capable workspaces and multi-device information consistency.

--- actions
[Back to projects](/en/#products) · [Português](/projects/pt/velis/)

:::

::: section architecture
## Architecture overview

Velis is a workspace for documents, tasks, structured notes and collaboration. Its architectural center is the local-first model: users can create and manipulate information locally while synchronization coordinates changes across connected clients.

- **Client experience:** editing, navigation and workspace interactions.
- **Local data layer:** durable device-side state and pending changes.
- **Synchronization layer:** orderly exchange of local updates and server changes.
- **Application services:** collaboration permissions, workspace operations and integrations.
- **Shared persistence and events:** authoritative service-side state and collaboration notifications.

Local persistence and real-time updates have complementary responsibilities rather than being interchangeable.
:::

::: section engineering
## Engineering decisions

A local-first architecture improves continuity when connectivity is unstable. Synchronization must account for retries, duplicate deliveries and conflicting edits. The editing layer uses operation-oriented concepts and keeps document manipulation separate from transport concerns.

The service architecture also distinguishes workspace membership from actions on particular resources. Only these conceptual boundaries are published; internal permission policies and synchronization mechanics remain private.
:::

::: section alternatives
## Alternatives and trade-offs

A server-first design reduces local-state complexity but depends more heavily on consistent connectivity. Whole-document replacement is simpler to implement, yet more prone to overwrite conflicts. Operation-based collaboration can support finer-grained updates, but it requires carefully defined convergence properties and a clear user experience when edits conflict.

An established collaboration framework can reduce implementation risk but may constrain storage and editor contracts. These are relevant alternatives, not a reconstructed history of project decisions.
:::

::: section verification
## Quality strategy and evidence

The private repository contains tests covering portions of synchronization behavior, conflict cases and convergence of concurrent editing operations. Those source tests were inspected, not re-run for this public documentation.

Further validation should combine randomized concurrent edit sequences, offline/online transition tests, multi-browser scenarios, schema evolution and data-loss checks. No specific synchronization latency, consistency guarantee across all features or production uptime is claimed here.
:::

::: section constraints
## Constraints and roadmap

Device storage capacity, stale data, large documents, long offline periods and simultaneous edits are important engineering constraints. The next architectural challenge is maintaining predictable behavior while expanding collaboration features and workload scale. Internal implementation details and specific operational weaknesses are excluded from this public summary.
:::

